WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.485
A TypeScript service should usually depend
on a

2
00:00:03.485 --> 00:00:06.283
credential-provider abstraction supplied
by its platform SDK.

3
00:00:06.283 --> 00:00:09.267
The provider knows how to obtain
credentials for

4
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:12.252
that environment and, where supported,
renew them.

5
00:00:12.252 --> 00:00:16.076
Business logic should not repeatedly
inspect credential files, copy tokens

6
00:00:16.076 --> 00:00:20.086
into configuration objects, or implement
its own token exchange protocol.

7
00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:23.071
The code example expresses this separation
with a

8
00:00:23.071 --> 00:00:26.242
conceptual default provider passed to a
service client.

9
00:00:26.242 --> 00:00:30.253
These names describe an architecture, not
a particular vendor API.

10
00:00:30.253 --> 00:00:32.118
Some SDKs choose a provider

11
00:00:32.118 --> 00:00:34.263
automatically; others require explicit
construction.

12
00:00:34.263 --> 00:00:37.621
Keep the provider attached to the client
so it

13
00:00:37.621 --> 00:00:40.699
can acquire fresh credentials when needed,
rather than

14
00:00:40.699 --> 00:00:44.243
resolving it once and retaining an
expiring token forever.

15
00:00:44.243 --> 00:00:46.482
Default chains often support local
developer

16
00:00:46.482 --> 00:00:48.627
sign-in, configuration files, environment
credentials,

17
00:00:48.627 --> 00:00:51.145
workload federation, and platform identity
endpoints.

18
00:00:51.145 --> 00:00:53.570
The exact sources, ordering, and failure

19
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.368
behavior vary by platform and SDK version.

20
00:00:56.368 --> 00:00:59.912
Document the chain that your deployed
application actually uses.

21
00:00:59.912 --> 00:01:03.457
An environment key may take precedence
over workload identity.

22
00:01:03.457 --> 00:01:07.187
A credential file copied into an image may
cause production

23
00:01:07.187 --> 00:01:10.731
to authenticate as a developer without
changing application code.

24
00:01:10.731 --> 00:01:13.809
Where ambiguity creates risk, configure
the intended production

25
00:01:13.809 --> 00:01:17.354
provider explicitly and make unavailable
identity an observable failure.

26
00:01:17.354 --> 00:01:20.152
Review provider options and deployment
settings together.

27
00:01:20.152 --> 00:01:22.390
Remove unused credential variables and
local

28
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.815
login files from the production runtime.

29
00:01:24.815 --> 00:01:27.427
Do not assume that configuring a managed

30
00:01:27.427 --> 00:01:30.225
identity prevents an earlier source from
winning.

31
00:01:30.225 --> 00:01:33.209
Local development can use an approved
developer login

32
00:01:33.209 --> 00:01:36.007
or controlled impersonation flow with
development permissions.

33
00:01:36.007 --> 00:01:39.365
That human-backed access is distinct from
the production workload

34
00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:43.003
identity, even when both use the same
client interface.

35
00:01:43.003 --> 00:01:45.241
Test the effective principal through
service

36
00:01:45.241 --> 00:01:48.039
audit records or a supported identity
endpoint.

37
00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:51.303
Record only approved, non-secret
identifiers and provider metadata.

38
00:01:51.303 --> 00:01:53.542
Never serialize the credential object or

39
00:01:53.542 --> 00:01:56.340
token to prove which account was selected.

